speechtext:
I did not intend to say anything on this question, but it appears to me there is one phase of it which my right hon. friend the Minister of Justice has not yet considered. The claim was made, I understand, against the city of Quebec that the city was responsible for the disorders at that time, that the city was responsible for the protection of the property and lives of the citizens. The claim was disallowed in the courts, I am told, because the civic authorities of Quebec were not in charge of keeping order at that time. That function was transferred from the city of Quebec to the military authorities. The Government at Ottawa took the very unusual step of passing an Order in Council for. that purpose, notwithstanding the fact that the civil authorities had not asked for any assistance from the military authorities.
I have here the Order in Council. It is somewhat lengthy, and I do not intend to do anything more than read a paragraph or two to illustrate the point I am trying to make. The Order in Council is dated April 4, 1918, and reads in parts as follows:

Whereas on the 28th day of March last the opposition of a large number of the population of the city of Quebec to the enforcement of the Military Service Act, 1917, and the regulations thereunder, broke out in open violence and serious riots ensued on that day and on subsequent days in which the rioters destroyed the office of the deputy registrar appointed for the District of Quebec under the said Act, and moreover caused great damage to other property, and threatened further damage, and it became necessary for the restoration of order, the protection of property and the preservation of the King's peace that these riots should be suppressed by military authority;
And whereas at common law it is the duty of a military officer with the troops under his command to interefere-when such interference is necessary, to put down riot, insurrection or civil disturbance ;
And whereas it is provided by the Militia Act that the active militia of Canada may be called out on active service in aid of the civil power upon requisition of the civil authorities, and the King's regulations and Orders for the Militia contain certain directions for regulating the exercise of command and disposition of the troops upon such occasions;
' And whereas notwithstanding that it was necessary as aforesaid for the preservation of the peace and to repress rioting that resort should be had to military force the civil authorities of Quebec made no requisition for the calling out of the Militia and the Officer Commanding therefore, acting manifestly in the general interest and for the protection of the public, and by direction of the Minister of Militia and Defence assumed the responsibility to command and direct the operation of the troops for the protection of life and property and the restoration of the peacein the city of Quebec. Now it appears that the military authorities intervened and took the city under their own protection, turning aside the civil authorities from looking after the property and lives of the citizens and the maintenance of peace and order. Consequently it follows that the military authorities should feel themselves responsible for what took place. If they had kept their hands off altogether and thrown the burden on the civic authorities, it would be another matter, but their interference at that stage and their taking upon themselves the duty of maintaining order in the city, puts the case in a different light. How did they perform tha't duty? The very men who were entrusted with the preservation of order in the city were the ones who committed the offences. As my hon. friend from West Quebec told us this afternoon, a shop was looted by soldiers wearing the uniform of His Majesty the King, and directed by officers of the Militia Department-the very men who were entrusted with the protection of the lives and property of the citizens of Quebec. Surely the responsibility is upon the Government, and not upon the city of Quebec, to compensate the people for the injuries that were done by these soldiers who did not perform their duty at that time. That was the decision of the courts, I understand, and I think the decision was right under the circumstances.
My right hon. friend the Minister of Justice says there is no legal claim. This is not put forward, I understand, as a strictly legal claim. It is a moral claim, and the practice in this Parliament, this High Court of Justice, has often been to entertain moral claims as well as legal claims. The claim of the city of Halifax was the moral claim. They had no legal claim upon us to furnish millions of money to compensate the victims of the disaster in the city of Halifax in December 1917, but the Government recognized that the disaster arose out of the war and that innocent victims in the city of Halifax had suffered great damage, and the Government stepped in and voted a large sum of money to compensate them.
I wish to draw that fact to the attention of my right hon. friend, not because the case is exactly the same, but because it disposes of the point made by him that he can deal only with cases of legal liability. We are not pressing for his attention in this case on any ground of legal liability. The question is as to whether it is just and right on the part of the Government to refuse to entertain these claims, seeing that they were responsible at the time for order in the city of Quebec and that the damage was done by soldiers in their service. This must not be forgotten. The men that did the damage were our soldiers, wearing our uniform, and directed by our officers. A case cited by my hon. friend from Quebec West seems to me to be a very serious one of hardship. The man in question was walking past the sentry. He did not understand English, although, when ordered to stop, he evidently realized the nature of the command and held up his hands. What more could he do? Nevertheless, a rifle was discharged and he was wounded, losing one of his limbs. Surely there is no excuse for that, if the facts be true. Whether they are strictly true or not is something that this House is not in a position to decide; it is a matter for my right hon. friend to find out. I understood from the minister's speech that he had made some investigation, but he did not tell us the results. He did not say whether these men injured in Quebec were in any way guilty of participation in the riots that took place in that city. That is a vital question. If they were not in any way connected with the riots, then I submit

that they have a moral claim for compensation. Of course, if they were associated with rioters and were making a disturbance, resisting the law, they would have no claim for compensation. But that appears to me to be the crux of the claim, and my right hon. friend did not tell the House whether he had discovered that they were guilty of any misconduct or not. I trust that this question will be considered on its merits, and considered with a view to doing justice to the victims of the riot. The cases put before us by the hon. member for Quebec West were clearly stated. We must put aside any question of legal liability, and I do not think that my hon. friend intends that the subject should be considered by the House as involving any legal claim. It is simply a matter in which justice ought to be done; as I have said, it is a moral claim. I hope that no discrimination will be made because of the fact that this trouble occurred in the city of Quebec. The case should be dealt with as though the incident had taken place in Toronto or in any other city in the Dominion.
Motion negatived on division.
